+### Task 2: Batch writes with `put_many`
+
+The job writes live records with `store.put_many` in batches of at most `MAX_BATCH` (25). The budget is checked before each batch request. A record counts as dealt with only once its batch is written, or, for a draft, once no unwritten live record comes before it. The cursor and the counts move only then.
+
+The rules the code must keep:
+- `pending` holds the records read since the last point the run dealt with. Its first element, if any, is always live. A draft read while `pending` is empty is dealt with at once: `skipped += 1` and the cursor moves to it. No request is made.
+- A draft read while `pending` is not empty is appended to `pending`. It is counted as skipped only when that batch is written. If the budget stops the run first, the next run reads it again, so counting it now would count it twice.
+- When `pending` holds `MAX_BATCH` live records, and again when the source runs out with `pending` not empty, `_flush` checks the budget and writes the batch. If the budget is spent it sets `stopped_by_budget` and returns `False`, and `run` returns without moving the cursor.
+- The next source page is read after the last record *read* (`after`), not after `result.cursor`. The cursor lags behind `pending`, so paging from it would re-read the same records forever.
+- The budget is checked only before a request. A run whose remaining records are all drafts, or whose last batch uses the last request, completes with `stopped_by_budget` false.
+
